  'Given class `SplayTree` definition:

```cpp
class SplayTree {
    struct Node {
        int val;
        Node* pLeft;
        Node* pRight;
        Node* pParent;
        Node(int val = 0, Node* l = nullptr, Node* r = nullptr, Node* par = nullptr) : val(val), pLeft(l), pRight(r), pParent(par)
    { }
    };
    Node* root;

    // print the tree structure for local testing
    void printBinaryTree(string prefix, const Node* root, bool isLeft, bool hasRightSibling) {
        if (!root && isLeft && hasRightSibling) {
            cout << prefix << "|\n";
        }
        if (!root) return;
        cout << prefix;
        if (isLeft && hasRightSibling)
            cout << "|—";
        else
            cout << "L—";
        cout << root->val << ''\n'';
        printBinaryTree(prefix + (isLeft && hasRightSibling ? "| " : " "), root->pLeft, true, root->pRight);
        printBinaryTree(prefix + (isLeft && hasRightSibling ? "| " : " "), root->pRight, false, root->pRight);
    }

    void printPreorder(Node* p) {
        if (!p) {
            return;
        }
        cout << p->val << '' '';
        printPreorder(p->pLeft);
        printPreorder(p->pRight);
    }

public:
    SplayTree() {
        root = nullptr;
    }
    ~SplayTree() {
        // Ignore deleting all nodes in the tree
    }

    void printBinaryTree() {
        printBinaryTree("", root, false, false);
    }

    void printPreorder() {
        printPreorder(root);
        cout << "\n";
    }

    void splay(Node* p) {
        // To Do
    }

    void insert(int val) {
        // To Do
    }
};
```

Implement the following methods:

1. `void splay(Node* p)`: bottom-up splaying a Node

When a splay operation is performed on Node `p`, it will be moved to the root. To perform a splay operation, carry out a sequence of splay steps, each of which moves `p` closer to the root.

The three types of splay steps are:

- Zig step
  *(Diagram showing transformation: Zig: small target large T1 T2 T3 becomes target small large T1 T2 T3)*

- Zig-zig step:
  *(Diagram showing transformation: Zig-zig: small target middle large T1 T2 T3 T4 becomes target small middle large T1 T2 T3 T4)*

- Zig-zag step:
  *(Diagram showing transformation: Zig-zag: small middle target large T1 T2 T3 T4 becomes target middle small large T1 T2 T3 T4)*

Note: there are also zag, zag-zag and zag-zig steps but they are not shown here.

2. `void insert(int val)`:

To insert a value `val` into a splay tree:

- Insert `val` as with a normal binary search tree.
- When the new value is inserted, perform a splay operation. As a result, the newly inserted node becomes the root of the tree.

Note: In a splay tree, the values in the left subtree <= the root''s value <= the values in the right subtree. In this exercise, when inserting a duplicate value, insert it into the right subtree to pass the test cases.

Constraint of test cases:

- number of operation <= $10^4$
- $1 \le val \le 10^5$

For example:

| Test | Input | Result |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| ```cpp
SplayTree tree;
int query;
cin >> query;
for(int i = 0; i < query; i++) {
    string op;
    int val;
    cin >> op >> val;
    if (op == "insert")
        tree.insert(val);
}
// print preorder traversal of the tree
tree.printPreorder();
// print structure of the tree
tree.printBinaryTree();
``` | 6<br>insert 50<br>insert 70<br>insert 30<br>insert 80<br>insert 100<br>insert 90 | 90 80 30 70 50 100<br>└─90<br>│ └─80<br>│   └─30<br>│   │ └─70<br>│   │     └─50<br>│   └─100 |
| ```cpp
SplayTree tree;
int query;
cin >> query;
for(int i = 0; i < query; i++) {
    string op;
    int val;
    cin >> op >> val;
    if (op == "insert")
        tree.insert(val);
}
// print preorder traversal of the tree
tree.printPreorder();
// print structure of the tree
tree.printBinaryTree();
``` | 6<br>insert 95<br>insert 200<br>insert 80<br>insert 100<br>insert 200<br>insert 95 | 95 95 80 200 100 200<br>└─95<br>│ └─95<br>│   └─80<br>│   │ └─200<br>│   │   └─100<br>│   └─200 |', 3
);

